   155	**2** **PARTIAL MAX-SAT FOR MOORE MACHINES**
   156	
   157	**2.1** **Moore Machines**
   158	
   159	
   160	Our algorithm assumes that the system we want to learn is deterministic and can be modelled as a Moore machine, as first described
   161	by Moore [ 33 ]. These are a type of FSM which can classically be
   162	defined as a 6-tuple, where the Moore machine M is:
   163	
   164	
   165	M = ( _𝐼,𝑂,𝑆,𝑠_ 0 _,𝛿_ M _, 𝜆_ M )
   166	
   167	where _𝐼_, _𝑂_ and _𝑆_ are the finite, non-empty sets of input symbols,
   168	output symbols and states, respectively, _𝑠_ 0 ∈ _𝑆_ is the initial state
   169	and _𝛿_ M : _𝑆_ × _𝐼_ → _𝑆_ and _𝜆_ M : _𝑆_ → _𝑂_ are the state transition
   170	and the output function, respectively. Importantly, the output function depends solely on the current state of the machine. A Moore
   171	machine is _input-complete_ if _𝛿_ M is fully defined.
   172	We define a _trace_ _𝑡_ to be _𝑡_ = ⟨ _𝑜_ 0 _,_ ( _𝑖_ 1 _,𝑜_ 1 ) _,_ ( _𝑖_ 2 _,𝑜_ 2 ) _, . . .,_ ( _𝑖_ _𝑚_ _,𝑜_ _𝑚_ )⟩
   173	with _𝑜_ _𝑗_ ∈ _𝑂_ and _𝑖_ _𝑗_ ∈ _𝐼_ . Note that the index of each trace starts with
   174	0, as the first element of the trace is the initial output _𝑜_ 0, which is the
   175	output of the initial state _𝑠_ 0, followed by _𝑚_ steps, i.e., input-output
   176	pairs, hence the length of the trace | _𝑡_ | = _𝑚_ + 1. For a given index
   177	_𝑘_ ∈[ 1 _,𝑚_ ] we will write _𝑖_ _𝑘_ and _𝑜_ _𝑘_ for the _𝑘_ _[𝑡ℎ]_ input and output of a
   178	trace, respectively.
   179	Additionally, we define a _non-deterministic_ Moore machine to
   180	use a transition relation _𝛿_ M : _𝑆_ × _𝐼_ × _𝑆_ instead of a function. If the
   181	transitions of a non-deterministic Moore machine are also labelled
   182	
   183	with their occurrence frequencies or probabilities, we obtain a
   184	_stochastic_ Moore machine. Note that the latter corresponds to a
   185	Markov-decision process.
   186	
   187	
   188	**2.2** **Partial Max-SAT**
   189	
   190	
   191	The aim of traditional SAT solving is to find an assignment of
   192	variables for a given set of clauses in conjunctive normal form
   193	(CNF) such that the formula becomes true, i.e., satisfiable, or to
   194	find that such an assignment does not exists, i.e., the formula is
   195	unsatisfiable. Partial Max-SAT (PMSAT) is a mixture of the SAT
   196	and Max-SAT problems, insofar as a PMSAT problem consists of
   197	two sets of clauses: one which has to be satisfied using SAT and
   198	one for which the number of fulfilled clauses has to be _maximised_
   199	using Max-SAT. The clauses in the former set are called _hard clauses_
   200	while the ones in the latter are called _soft clauses_ . Solving a PMSAT
   201	problem comes down to finding an assignment that satisfies the
   202	maximum number of soft clauses while also satisfying all hard
   203	clauses [4, 14].
   204	
   205	
   206	_Quantifiers._ To more concisely describe the multitude of clauses
   207	necessary to build the PMSAT problem we use quantifiers. We
   208	resolve all quantifiers through enumeration such that ∀ _𝑥_ ∈ _𝑋_ :
   209	P( _𝑥_ ) corresponds to the CNF formula P( _𝑥_ 1 ) ∧P ( _𝑥_ 2 ) ∧ _. . ._ ∧P( _𝑥_ _𝑛_ ),
   210	where each P( _𝑥_ _𝑗_ ) is a propositional variable and P( _𝑥_ _𝑗_ ) its negation.
   211	
   212	
   213	
   214	_Exactly One._ We define ∃ ! _𝑥_ ∈ _𝑋_ : P( _𝑥_ ) to mean that we require
   215	exactly one of the _𝑛_ variables {P( _𝑥_ ) : _𝑥_ ∈ _𝑋_ } to be true, which will
   216	result in the following clauses: (P( _𝑥_ 1 ) ∨P( _𝑥_ 2 ) ∨ _. . ._ ∨P( _𝑥_ _𝑛_ )) to require at least one variable to be true and ∀ _𝑖_ ∈[ 1 _,𝑛_ − 1 ] _,_ ∀ _𝑗_ ∈[ _𝑖_ + 1 _,𝑛_ ] :
   217	(P( _𝑥_ _𝑖_ ) ∨P( _𝑥_ _𝑗_ )) to prevent any pair-wise combination of variables
   218	to be true at the same time. For example, ∃ ! _𝑥_ ∈ { _𝑥_ 1 _,𝑥_ 2 _,𝑥_ 3 } : P( _𝑥_ )
   219	would result in (P( _𝑥_ 1 ) ∨P( _𝑥_ 2 ) ∨P( _𝑥_ 3 )) ∧(P( _𝑥_ 1 ) ∨P( _𝑥_ 2 )) ∧
   220	(P( _𝑥_ 1 ) ∨P( _𝑥_ 3 )) ∧(P( _𝑥_ 2 ) ∨P( _𝑥_ 3 )).
