     1	# Partial Max-SAT Inference Algorithm and Publication Artifacts
     2	
     3	This repository contains the source code of the implementation used for the [PMSAT inference algorithm](pmsatlearn/).
     4	It also contains the scripts used to generate benchmark sets and run the uses cases presented in the paper "[It's Not a Feature, It's a Bug: Fault-Tolerant Model Mining from Noisy Data](https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3623346)" by [Felix Wallner](https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8129-9928), [Bernhard Aichernig](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3484-5584) and [Christian Burghard](https://orcid.org/0009-0009-3246-4399) accepted to the [ICSE2024 Conference](https://conf.researchr.org/home/icse-2024).
     5	
     6	> The PMSAT inference algorithm is actively maintained on [gitlab.com/felixwallner/pmsat-inference](https://gitlab.com/felixwallner/pmsat-inference)
     7	
     8	## Dependencies
     9	
    10	This library requires Python 3.10 or newer.
    11	
    12	Additionally, it requires the following packages:
    13	
    14	* [AALpy](https://github.com/DES-Lab/AALpy)
    15	* [PySAT](https://github.com/pysathq/pysat)
    16	* [Pebble](https://pythonhosted.org/Pebble/)
    17	
    18	Please install the dependencies with:
    19	```
    20	pip install -r requirements.txt
    21	```
    22	
    23	## PMSAT Inference Algorithm
    24	
    25	The source code of the actual inference algorithm lives in `pmsatlearn`.
    26	It can be used as follows:
    27	
    28	```python
    29	from pmsatlearn import run_pmSATLearn
    30	
    31	traces = [
    32	    ["offline", ("disconnect", "offline"), ("connect", "online")],
    33	    ["offline", ("connect", "online"), ("connect", "online"), ("disconnect", "offline")],
    34	    ["offline", ("disconnect", "offline"), ("connect", "offline")],
    35	]
    36	n = 2 # the number of states of the inferred automaton
    37	pmstrategy = "rc2" # can be "lsu", "fm" or "rc2"
    38	
    39	deterministic_moore, stochastic_moore, info = run_pmSATLearn(
    40	    traces,
    41	    n,
    42	    "moore",
    43	    pm_strategy=pmstrategy,
    44	    timeout=None,
    45	    print_info=True,
    46	)
    47	
    48	deterministic_moore.visualize("deterministic")
    49	stochastic_moore.visualize("stochastic") # glitched transitions have the "!" prefix
    50	```
    51	
    52	## PMSAT Inference Helper
    53	
    54	You may also directly run PMSAT inference on a set of traces in JSON format providing either `n` for a specific number of states or `nmax` to infer all automata up to that given number of states:
    55	```
    56	python3 run_pmsat_on_traces.py use_cases/avl415SE_smokemeter/smokemeter_trace.json -nmax 15
    57	```
    58	
    59	Alternatively, you may load a Moore machine in DOT format, then generate traces on the fly and inject glitches by dropping a certain percentage of steps from the traces:
    60	```
    61	python3 run_pmsat_on_moore.py use_cases/ble_nRF52832/nRF52832_moore_without_mtu_req.dot
    62	```
    63	
    64	## Use Cases, Benchmarking Sets and Analysis
    65	
    66	Use cases can be found in `use_cases`.
    67	These include the traces used for learning the Smoke Meter and the APC, as well as the traces and scripts used to parse the BLE device outputs.
    68	
    69	For use cases and examples, PDF-files were also already generated and added to the relevant folders.
    70	For example, the correctly inferred models for each use case can be found here:
    71	
    72	* [APC](use_cases/avl_APC/learning-results/pmsatLearned-rc2-N9-STOC.pdf)
    73	* [Smoke Meter](use_cases/avl415SE_smokemeter/learning-results/pmsatLearned-rc2-N11-STOC.pdf)
    74	* [BLE nRF52832](use_cases/ble_nRF52832/learning-results/pmsatLearned-rc2-N14-STOC.pdf)
    75	
    76	Two benchmarking sets were used in the paper:
    77	
    78	* `benchmarkingset-lsu-fm-rc2-results` contains the 500 experiments each used by the three different PMSAT algorithms.
    79	* `benchmarkingset-rc2-results` contains the larger benchmarking set only for RC2 for the performance analysis.
    80	* `benchmarkingset-no-optimisation-results` contains the identical benchmarking set as RC2 (5000 experiments) run with **no optimisation** (without optimisation Equation 8).
    81	* `benchmarkingset-trace-results` contains the benchmarks with extended traces in order to analyse performance w.r.t. trace steps.
    82	* `benchmarkingset-faults-results` contains the benchmarks with different fault types in order to analyse performance w.r.t. fault type.
    83	* `benchmarkingset-o5-g1` contains the benchmarks for the IO ALERGIA comparison in Figure 3e.
    84	
    85	The results and automata used in the examples are found in `examples-results`, which can be run and recreated with `run_pmsat_on_example.py`.
    86	
    87	The benchmarking sets can be recreated - or new ones generated - with the `benchmark*.py` scripts. (Except for "no-optimisation", which has to manually be diabled in `pmsatlearn/learnalgo.py`)
    88	This may take up to several hours or days.
    89	
    90	The benchmarking results can be evaluated and plots generated with:
    91	```
    92	python3 parse_all_results.py benchmarkingset-rc2-results
    93	```
    94	(parameters and accumulators have to be set in the script directly)
    95	
    96	Evaluating a single series of experiments with different `n` can be done with:
    97	```
    98	python3 parse_single_run_results.py examples-results/ping_pong_example
    99	```
   100	
   101	Similarly, the different types of faults can be evaluated like so:
   102	
   103	```
   104	python3 parse_all_results_faults.py benchmarkingset-faults-results
   105	```
   106	
   107	And the IO ALERGIA comparison can be run with (which will also run IO ALERGIA on the fly):
   108	```
   109	python3 evaluate_results_alergia_fscore.py benchmarkingset-o5-g1
   110	```
   111	
   112	The analysis of the trace length benchmarks including drawing the box plots can be done with:
   113	```
   114	python3 parse_all_results_trace.py benchmarkingset-trace-results
   115	```
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	Finally, to quickly compare two Moore machines use `compare_models.py` like so: 
   120	
   121	```
   122	python3 compare_models.py use_cases/ble_nRF52832/nRF52832_moore_without_mtu_req.dot use_cases/ble_nRF52832/learning-results/pmsatLearned-rc2-N13.dot
   123	
   124	> CEX for bisimilarity: ['connection_req', 'feature_rsp', 'version_req']
   125	
   126	python3 compare_models.py use_cases/ble_nRF52832/nRF52832_moore_without_mtu_req.dot use_cases/ble_nRF52832/learning-results/pmsatLearned-rc2-N14.dot
   127	
   128	> Automata are bisimilar!
   129	```
   130	
   131	or alternativly calculate the F1-score with `f_similarity.py` between two automata to see how similar their languages are:
   132	
   133	```
   134	python3 f_similarity.py benchmarkingset-faults-results/n_states=5-fault_type=\'swap\'/725893a28cec258d4e588cd177712023/RealModel.dot benchmarkingset-faults-results/n_states=5-fault_type=\'swap\'/725893a28cec258d4e588cd177712023/pmsatLearned-N5.dot
   135	
   136	> precision=0.9343, recall=0.9346, f_score=0.9344499759216652
   137	
   138	python3 f_similarity.py benchmarkingset-faults-results/n_states=5-fault_type=\'swap\'/725893a28cec258d4e588cd177712023/RealModel.dot benchmarkingset-faults-results/n_states=5-fault_type=\'swap\'/725893a28cec258d4e588cd177712023/alergiaLearned.dot -to mdp
   139	
   140	> precision=0.4577, recall=1.0, f_score=0.6279755779652878
   141	```
   142	
   143	Similarly, a Moore machine can be compared to a trace to quickly see how well the traces fit the automaton using `compare_models_to_trace.py`:
   144	
   145	```
   146	python3 compare_model_to_trace.py examples-results/simple_example_server_with_glitch/RealModel.dot examples-results/simple_example_server_with_glitch/info.json
   147	```
   148	
   149	## Citing this Project
   150	
   151	If you make use of this algorithm or the provided publication artifacts, please [cite](https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3623346) us.
   152	
   153	


